WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.947 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The Console shows forty-two point zero.

00:00:03.167 --> 00:00:05.407 line:0 position:50% size:95%
But the GPU does not print that line.

00:00:05.627 --> 00:00:10.021 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Follow the value from the kernel to device memory, then back to the CPU.

00:00:12.041 --> 00:00:17.033 line:0 position:50% size:95%
On this simplified discrete GPU, the compute chip and device memory are separate.

00:00:17.253 --> 00:00:19.728 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The CPU reserves space for one float.

00:00:19.948 --> 00:00:25.089 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The pointer output identifies that space; allocating it does not execute hello.

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:30.992 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The CPU submits a launch: one block containing one thread.

00:00:31.212 --> 00:00:33.473 line:0 position:50% size:95%
These numbers describe logical work.

00:00:33.693 --> 00:00:37.875 line:0 position:50% size:95%
They do not select a physical processor or reserve the whole GPU.

00:00:39.895 --> 00:00:44.865 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The block is assigned to a streaming multiprocessor, or SM, on the compute chip.

00:00:45.085 --> 00:00:48.285 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Its execution hardware runs the thread's instructions.

00:00:48.505 --> 00:00:52.580 line:0 position:50% size:95%
A thread is an execution of code, not a dedicated piece of hardware.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:59.357 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The assignment stores forty-two point zero at the address for output index zero.

00:00:59.577 --> 00:01:02.265 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The value belongs to the GPU allocation.

00:01:02.485 --> 00:01:05.152 line:0 position:50% size:95%
It is not yet the CPU variable value.

00:01:05.372 --> 00:01:08.017 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Cache details are omitted from this data path.

00:01:10.037 --> 00:01:15.755 line:0 position:50% size:95%
This device-to-host copy waits for the result and places it in the CPU variable value.

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:19.900 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The launch statement by itself did not mean the kernel had already finished.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.032 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Now main prints its CPU copy.

00:01:24.252 --> 00:01:28.433 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The SM executed the assignment, device storage held the result,

00:01:28.653 --> 00:01:31.725 line:0 position:50% size:95%
and the copy made that result available to the CPU.

00:01:31.945 --> 00:01:33.631 line:0 position:50% size:95%
These are three different roles.

00:01:35.651 --> 00:01:39.576 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Pause and predict: if the assignment stores thirteen point zero,

00:01:39.796 --> 00:01:41.609 line:0 position:50% size:95%
does the launch need more threads?

00:01:41.829 --> 00:01:44.731 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Which value will the CPU print after the copy?

00:01:50.551 --> 00:01:52.428 line:0 position:50% size:95%
The launch still requests one thread.

00:01:52.648 --> 00:01:54.589 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Only the stored value changes.

00:01:54.809 --> 00:01:59.887 line:0 position:50% size:95%
In hello in the CUDA source editor, set the stored value to thirteen point zero f.

00:02:00.107 --> 00:02:02.752 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Click Compile and run, and inspect Console.

00:02:02.972 --> 00:02:06.215 line:0 position:50% size:95%
Leave the allocation, copy and error checks unchanged.
